taxPOD Ask An Expert — Answer Format Guide
This guide sets the standard format for every answer in the Answer Bank, so answers look and read the same whatever tax category they fall under. It is based on a review of the 535 existing answers (column E of Answer Bank Format.xlsx). It keeps what most answers already do and settles the points where they differ.
---
1. Universal skeleton (every answer, every category)
```
Dear Valued Customer,

[A. Opening line — one sentence]

[B. Assumptions / understanding — only the facts that would change the treatment]

1. [Subtitle — issue or tax type]
[Explanation in prose → authority quoted → application to facts → conclusion]

2. [Subtitle]
...

[C. Recommendation / next step — optional, one short paragraph]

Disclaimer
[Standard disclaimer — Section 6]

Thank you.
```
A. Salutation
Use	Do not use
`Dear Valued Customer,`	Dear Valued Client, / Dear Team, / Dear Valued Colleague, / Dear, / no comma
`尊敬的客户，` (Chinese answers, full-width comma)	尊敬的客户, (half-width comma)
B. Opening line (pick one)
`Please find our comments below.` (default)
`We refer to your query on [topic in 5–10 words].` (when the question is long or covers several issues)
`Further to your clarification on [point], our further comments are as follows.` (follow-up answers; "REFER TO THE PREVIOUS QUESTION" rows)
Do not restate the customer's scenario. Go straight to the points that matter.
C. Assumptions / understanding
Include this block only when the answer depends on facts the customer did not confirm. List only the facts that would change the tax treatment.
```
Our comments are based on the following assumptions:
- [fact 1 that drives the outcome];
- [fact 2]; and
- [fact 3].
Please let us know if any of the above is incorrect, as the tax treatment may differ.
```
Use a single sentence if there is only one assumption: "Our comments assume that [X]."
D. Body sections
Number the sections `1.`, `2.`, `3.` and give each a short subtitle — the issue or the tax type (e.g. `1. Withholding Tax ("WHT")`, `2. Is a self-billed e-Invoice required?`).
Use lettered sub-sections `A.`, `B.` only when a numbered section has two or more distinct limbs (e.g. A. Stamp Duty Implications / B. Income Tax Implications).
For a short single-issue answer (under about 150 words), subtitles may be left out, but the order inside still applies.
Within each section, follow this order:
Principle — the general rule in one or two sentences.
Authority — cite and quote it (Section 5).
Application — apply the rule to the customer's facts.
Conclusion — one clear sentence that starts with "Accordingly, …" or "Therefore, …".
E. Prose vs. point form
Write in smooth prose by default.
Use point form only for conditions, criteria, document lists, or facts relied on.
In a list, end each item with `;`, put `and` / `or` after the second-last item, and end the last item with `.`
F. Sign-off
English: `Thank you.` — on its own line, after the disclaimer. Do not use variants ("Thank you for your understanding.", etc.).
Chinese: `谢谢。`
---
2. Defined terms and house style
Define each term in full the first time it appears, then use the abbreviation:
First use	Thereafter
Income Tax Act 1967 ("ITA 1967")	ITA 1967
Inland Revenue Board of Malaysia ("IRBM")	IRBM — do not mix with LHDN / HASiL / IRB in the same answer
Royal Malaysian Customs Department ("RMCD")	RMCD
Public Ruling No. X/YYYY ("PR X/YYYY")	PR X/YYYY
Withholding Tax ("WHT")	WHT
Real Property Gains Tax ("RPGT")	RPGT
Sales and Service Tax ("SST")	SST
Transfer Pricing ("TP") / Transfer Pricing Documentation ("TPD")	TP / TPD
Year of Assessment ("YA")	YA2026 (no space)
Other style points:
Write amounts as `RM10,000`, `USD5,000` (no space). Write percentages as `8%`.
Write e-Invoice as `e-Invoice` (not einvoice / E-invoice).
Use measured wording such as "would generally", "appears to" and "in our view". Do not use "definitely" or "100%".
Do not name the customer's counterparties unless needed. When needed, use the name the customer used.
---
3. Multi-tax rule (replaces "Other Tax Matters" / "Others")
When a question touches more than one tax:
Give each tax its own numbered section, titled by tax type.
Put the sections in this fixed order (skip any that do not apply):
Income Tax (deductibility / taxability)
Withholding Tax
Sales and Service Tax (incl. Imported Taxable Service)
e-Invoice
Stamp Duty
RPGT / Capital Gains Tax
Transfer Pricing
Non-tax matters (Companies Act, accounting, HR) — flag these briefly and refer to the relevant professional
Use one Assumptions block at the top for all taxes, not one per section.
Use one combined disclaimer at the end that names every tax covered (Section 6).
Each section follows its own category template below.
Tagging: In the Answer Bank, set Category to the main tax issue. Record the other taxes covered in Tags. Use "Other Tax Matters" only for questions outside all the categories below (e.g. tax administration, MyTax / CP forms, audits, penalties, appeals).
---
4. Category templates
Each template shows the section order and a checklist of assumptions that typically change the treatment — use it to choose what goes in the Assumptions block.
4.1 Income Tax
Typical sections: `1. Tax treatment for the Company` → `2. Tax treatment for the employee / individual` (if relevant) → `3. Supporting documents`
Authorities: ITA 1967 (s.4, s.13, s.33, s.39, s.24, Schedule 3, Schedule 6), relevant Public Rulings, P.U.(A) Rules / Orders.
Assumptions that change the outcome:
revenue vs capital nature;
wholly and exclusively incurred in the production of gross income;
whether the recipient is an employee / director / shareholder with control;
basis period and YA concerned; and
residence status.
Conclusion line format:
`Accordingly, the [expense/receipt] of RM[x] would generally be [deductible / not deductible / taxable / exempt] under [provision].`
Benefits-in-kind / perquisites answers must say how the item is reported on Form EA (Part / item) where relevant.
4.2 Withholding Tax
Typical sections: `1. Whether WHT applies (nature of payment)` → `2. Applicable section and rate` → `3. DTA relief` (if any) → `4. Payment timeline & e-Invoice / ITS interaction` → `5. Supporting documents`
Authorities: ITA 1967 s.4A, s.15, s.15A, s.107A, s.109, s.109B, s.109F, s.39(1)(f); PR 10/2019; relevant Practice Notes; the applicable DTA article.
Assumptions that change the outcome:
payee's residence and whether it has a place of business in Malaysia;
where the services are physically performed (in Malaysia or outside);
whether the payment is for the use of, or the right to use, IP, software, databases or know-how (royalty);
whether a DTA applies and a Certificate of Residence is available; and
whether disbursements / reimbursements are included.
State in every answer: the category of payment, the section, the rate, the due date (within one month after paying or crediting), and the consequence of non-compliance (s.39(1)(f) disallowance / increase).
4.3 Stamp Duty
Typical sections: `1. Whether the document is a chargeable instrument` → `2. Applicable item and duty` → `3. Exemption / remission` (if any) → `4. Party liable, due date & late stamping penalty` → `5. Recommendation`
Authorities: Stamp Act 1949 (s.4, s.36, s.47A, First Schedule items), Stamp Duty (Exemption/Remission) Orders, IRBM Stamp Duty Guidelines, Self-Assessment Stamp Duty Guidelines.
Assumptions that change the outcome:
whether the document has been reviewed in full or only by its title;
whether the document creates enforceable rights and obligations (vs. a quotation / PO / booking);
the nature of the consideration (ad valorem vs fixed RM10);
execution date (in Malaysia vs abroad); and
the parties' relationship.
Always add (where the full document was not reviewed): "Stamp duty is imposed on the instrument and is determined by its substance and legal effect, rather than its title." Recommend adjudication when certainty is needed.
4.4 e-Invoicing
Typical sections: `1. Whether an e-Invoice is required` → `2. Type of e-Invoice (normal / consolidated / self-billed)` → `3. Timing of issuance` → `4. Key fields / classification code` → `5. Interaction with WHT / SST` (if relevant)
Authorities: ITA 1967 s.82C; Income Tax (Electronic Invoice) Rules; e-Invoice Guideline; e-Invoice Specific Guideline (quote paragraph no.); e-Invoice General FAQ (quote FAQ no.).
Assumptions that change the outcome:
annual turnover / implementation phase;
buyer is a business vs individual consumer;
foreign vs local supplier / buyer;
transaction value (RM10,000 threshold for consolidation); and
whether a specific exemption or relaxation applies.
Always cite the guideline paragraph or FAQ number — not just "the e-Invoice guideline".
4.5 Sales and Service Tax
Typical sections: `1. Whether the service / goods is taxable (Group & item)` → `2. Applicable rate` → `3. Exemption / exclusion` → `4. Registration / ITS accounting` → `5. Supporting documents`
Authorities: Service Tax Act 2018, Service Tax Regulations 2018 (First Schedule Groups), Sales Tax Act 2018, Sales Tax (Rates of Tax) Order, Sales Tax (Goods Exempted) Order, RMCD Guides and Policies (quote paragraph no.).
Assumptions that change the outcome:
whether the provider is registered;
whether the service is a standalone service or part of a composite supply;
B2B vs B2C;
whether the supplier is foreign and whether it has charged service tax; and
date of supply relative to effective dates (e.g. 1 July 2025 expansion).
State in every answer: the Group (e.g. Group K – Rental or Leasing Services) and the rate.
4.6 RPGT / Capital Gains Tax
Typical sections: `1. Whether the disposal is chargeable` → `2. Disposal price & acquisition price` → `3. Rate (by disposer type and holding period)` → `4. Exemptions / reliefs` → `5. Filing & retention requirements`
Authorities: RPGT Act 1976 (Schedule 2 incl. para 34A, Schedule 5); RPGT Exemption Orders; ITA 1967 Part IX (CGT on unlisted shares).
Assumptions that change the outcome:
disposer type (citizen / PR individual, non-citizen, company);
acquisition and disposal dates;
whether the company is an RPC (75% test);
whether the gain is income vs capital (trading); and
whether the transfer is between family members / related parties.
When a computation is given, show it as a table: Disposal price − Acquisition price − Permitted expenses = Chargeable gain × Rate.
4.7 Transfer Pricing
Typical sections: `1. Whether the transaction is a controlled transaction` → `2. TP documentation requirement & threshold` → `3. Arm's length method / pricing` → `4. Benchmarking / comparables` → `5. Penalty & risk exposure`
Authorities: ITA 1967 s.140A; Income Tax (Transfer Pricing) Rules 2023; Malaysian Transfer Pricing Guidelines 2024 (quote Chapter / paragraph no.).
Assumptions that change the outcome:
whether the parties are associated persons;
gross business income and transaction value relative to the thresholds;
whether the transaction is a loan, a service or a reimbursement at cost; and
whether the parties are local only or cross-border.
