5. Citing and quoting authorities
Every answer must cite at least one authority, and the key operative wording must be quoted verbatim.
Format:
```
[Paragraph / Section reference] of [Full name of authority] states that:

"[verbatim operative wording]"

[One sentence explaining what this means for the customer.]
```
Examples of the reference line:
`Paragraph 10.1 of Public Ruling No. 10/2019 – Withholding Tax on Special Classes of Income states that:`
`Subsection 39(1)(f) of the ITA 1967 provides that:`
`Paragraph 3.2(c) of Service Tax Policy No. 4/2020 states that:`
`Item 4 of the First Schedule to the Stamp Act 1949 provides that:`
`FAQ No. 125 of the e-Invoice General FAQ states that:`
Rules:
Quote only the operative part, not the whole paragraph.
Keep the reference on the same line as the text. (In existing answers, references such as "Public Ruling No. 11/2019" often sit on their own line; this is a copy-paste artefact and should be fixed.)
For a Bahasa Malaysia source, quote it in BM and add an English gloss in brackets.
For a media statement or IRBM / RMCD FAQ, give the issuing body and the date.
Do not cite an authority you have not checked against the current version.
---
6. Disclaimer (mandatory — every answer)
Put the disclaimer immediately before `Thank you.`, under the heading `Disclaimer`.
Standard (default):
> Disclaimer
> The above comments are based on the information provided and the assumptions stated, and are intended for general guidance only. The actual [tax type(s)] treatment may differ depending on the specific facts, contractual terms and supporting documents. We recommend reviewing the relevant [agreement / invoice / documents] before reaching a final conclusion.
Stamp Duty variant (document not reviewed in full):
> Disclaimer
> Kindly note that our comments are based solely on the limited facts provided, without reviewing the full instrument. Stamp duty treatment is determined by the substance, legal effect and specific provisions of the instrument rather than its title. We recommend reviewing the executed document in full and, where necessary, seeking legal advice or applying for adjudication before reaching a definitive conclusion.
Multi-tax answers: use the standard disclaimer and list every tax covered, e.g. "The actual income tax, WHT and SST treatment may differ…".
Non-tax matters (HR, labour, Companies Act, accounting): add one sentence: "As the matter also involves [HR / legal / accounting] considerations, we recommend consulting the relevant professional for advice on those aspects."
